  "Question": "A large square with side length \\((a+b)\\) is formed from 4 right triangles with legs \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) and hypotenuse \\(c\\), together with a central small square with side length \\(c\\). Which equation expresses the area relationship?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\((a+b)^2=4\\times(ab/2)+c^2\\)",
    "B": "\\((a+b)^2=ab+c^2\\)",
    "C": "\\((a+b)^2=4ab+c^2\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The area \\((a+b)^2\\) of the large square equals the total area \\(4\\times(ab/2)\\) of the 4 triangles plus the area \\(c^2\\) of the small square. Expanding gives \\(a^2+2ab+b^2=2ab+c^2\\), so \\(a^2+b^2=c^2\\).",
